meta {
  name: Revoke
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/revoke
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  X-AzureBank-Token-Road: bff
}

body:json {
  {
    "refreshTokens": ["{{refreshToken}}"]
  }
}

docs {
  WHAT THIS IS. The call the BFF makes when one session ends: a sign-out, idle expiry, the cap, a
  new sign-in over an old cookie, or a graceful stop. It ends the grant login published and no
  other: the one register issued stays live until logout, the next request.

  No bearer token, because the grant is the credential. The service key, the token-road marker
  and a loopback caller still apply, as on the other four token endpoints.

  A grant already revoked, or one never issued, gets the same 200 (RFC 7009), so this proves the
  call is accepted, not that the grant ended. Presenting the grant to refresh afterwards would
  prove that, and would also trip the reuse tripwire on purpose, writing a security event and an
  audit row. The backend suite proves it instead, in AuthEndpointTests.
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("Status is 200 OK", function() {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("The revocation is acknowledged", function() {
    expect(res.body.message).to.equal('Revoked');
  });
}
